# 1. Make a 3D web page

A slide shows interactive 3D the way a browser does: as a web page. The first step is that page. Choose
the lowest step that is enough for what you want to show.

| What you have | What to do | Effort |
|---|---|---|
| A model file (OBJ, glTF) | Show it with a ready-made viewer | none to small |
| Your own WebGL code in JavaScript | It is already a web page | none |
| A C++ program in OpenGL 3.3 | Compile it to WebAssembly with Emscripten | small: four changes |
| A C++ program on a library (CGP, GLFW...) | Compile it the same way, if the library supports the web | small |

## A model file

A model that turns with the mouse needs no code to compile. Two well documented viewers:

- [model-viewer](https://modelviewer.dev/): one HTML tag shows a glTF file, with the mouse controls.
- [three.js](https://threejs.org/): a short page loads an OBJ or glTF file, with its `OrbitControls` for
  the mouse. A complete
  page, tested, with an image for the PDF: [a 3D model in a slide](https://scholartools.fr/lhtml/03_recipes/04_3d/index.html).

Then go to [step 2](../02_in-your-slides/index.html). The rest of this page is for your own program.

## Your C++ OpenGL program

[Emscripten](https://emscripten.org/) compiles C++ to WebAssembly, and OpenGL ES 3.0 calls to WebGL 2.
A program in OpenGL 3.3 core compiles for the web with four changes, marked with `#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__`.
The smallest example, a cube in about 200 lines: [A1]({{ repo }}/tree/main/examples/a1-opengl-cube).

1. **Headers:** `<GLES3/gl3.h>` on the web, your loader (glad) on the desktop.
2. **First line of the shaders:** `#version 300 es` and a `precision` on the web, `#version 330 core`
   on the desktop. Keep the body of the shaders the same and prepend the right line:

   ```cpp
   #ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
   char const* const shader_header = "#version 300 es\nprecision highp float;\n";
   #else
   char const* const shader_header = "#version 330 core\n";
   #endif
   char const* sources[] = {shader_header, body};
   glShaderSource(shader, 2, sources, nullptr);
   ```

3. **Size of the drawing:** the canvas of the page. Create the window at its size, otherwise
   `glfwCreateWindow` resizes the canvas, and read it at each frame:

   ```cpp
   emscripten_get_canvas_element_size("#canvas", &width, &height);
   ```

4. **Main loop:** the browser calls your frame function at each refresh, instead of a `while` loop:

   ```cpp
   #ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
   emscripten_set_main_loop(frame, 0, 1);
   #else
   while (!glfwWindowShouldClose(window)) frame();
   #endif
   ```

Compile it with the Emscripten SDK installed and activated:

```
emcmake cmake -S . -B build_web
cmake --build build_web
```

with these link options (GLFW of Emscripten, WebGL 2, and the two functions of the bridge of
[step 3](../03_drive/index.html)):

```
-sUSE_GLFW=3 -sMIN_WEBGL_VERSION=2 -sMAX_WEBGL_VERSION=2
-sEXPORTED_FUNCTIONS=_main,_slide_set,_slide_describe -sEXPORTED_RUNTIME_METHODS=ccall
```

The build gives `project.js` and `project.wasm`, and `project.data` when the program reads files
(shaders, models) packed with `--preload-file`.

### With a library

A library that supports the web compiles the same way. [CGP](https://github.com/drohmer/cgp-2027), made
for teaching computer graphics, has a web build: its template project runs in a slide without changing a
line ([C1]({{ repo }}/tree/main/examples/c1-cgp-template)), and [four examples](../../02_more/01_examples/index.html)
are CGP programs. GLFW and SDL2 programs use the ports of Emscripten (`-sUSE_GLFW=3`, `-sUSE_SDL=2`).

<div class="callout warn"><div class="callout-title">What WebGL does not have</div>

- `glPolygonMode` does not exist: a wireframe drawn with it shows nothing on the web, without an error.
  Draw the edges as lines (`GL_LINES`).
- Most browsers draw lines one pixel wide, whatever `glLineWidth` asks: a curve that must be seen from
  the back of the room is better drawn as a tube.
- No geometry shaders, no compute shaders (WebGL 2 is OpenGL ES 3.0).

</div>

## The page of the demo

The page that Emscripten writes is made for a browser window, not for a frame in a slide. Use
[slide-frame.html]({{ repo }}/blob/main/bridge/slide-frame.html) instead: copy it next to `project.js`,
as `index.html`. It

- fills the frame of the slide, whatever the size of the window the program creates;
- passes the settings of its address to the program (`index.html?speed=0.2` gives `--speed 0.2`);
- sends the arrow keys and Page Down / Page Up to the slides, even after a click in the demo;
- fades the demo in once its first frames are drawn, and says when it is ready (for a PDF export);
- keeps the image of the canvas, so that it can be captured (a poster, tests).

```
demo/
  index.html      slide-frame.html, renamed
  project.js      built by Emscripten
  project.wasm
  project.data
```

<div class="callout"><div class="callout-title">A web server, not a file</div>

A browser refuses to load `project.wasm` from a page opened as a file (`file://`). Open the demo through a
web server: `python3 -m http.server` in its folder, then `http://localhost:8000/`. Your slides need one too
(step 2).

</div>

